+++
title: Osiris
description: Ancient Egyptian god of the afterlife
hook: Set killed his brother Osiris and cut him to pieces. Isis and her sister
  Nephthys searched Egypt for the parts and collected all but one — his
  genitalia; she wrapped the body up, and that was enough to bring him back to
  life.
standfirst: The first evidence of Osiris's worship comes from the middle of
  the Fifth Dynasty of Egypt, in the 25th century BCE, though he was likely
  worshipped much earlier, and one of his epithets goes back at least to the
  First Dynasty. Most of what is known of his myth is drawn from allusions,
  and from Greek authors writing much later.
key-facts:
  - name: Worshipped above all at
    value: Busiris, Abydos
  - name: Recognised by
    value: Crook and flail, Atef crown, ostrich feathers, fish, mummy gauze, djed
  - name: Born to
    value: Geb and Nut Ipy
  - name: Brother of
    value: Isis, Set, Nephthys, Horus the Elder
  - name: Married to
    value: Isis
  - name: Father of
    value: Horus the Younger, Anubis (in some sources)
pull-quotes:
  - text: gloomy, solemn, and mournful\...
    attribution: Plutarch, Isis and Osiris, 69, on the sacrifices to Osiris
    section: death-and-institution-as-god-of-the-dead
  - text: the priests bring forth a sacred chest containing a small golden coffer,
      into which they pour some potable water\...and a great shout arises from
      the company for joy that Osiris is found (or resurrected).
    attribution: Plutarch, Isis and Osiris, 39
    section: wheat-and-clay-rituals
  - text: beat their breasts and gashed their shoulders.... When they pretend that
      the mutilated remains of the god have been found and rejoined\...they
      turn from mourning to rejoicing.
    attribution: Julius Firmicus Maternus, De Errore Profanarum Religionum
    section: death-and-institution-as-god-of-the-dead
  - text: I pass over the cutting of the wood
    attribution: Plutarch, Isis and Osiris, 21
    section: wheat-and-clay-rituals
timeline:
  - when: at least the First Dynasty
    what: The Khenti-Amentiu epithet, later attached to Osiris, is already in use,
      and was used as a pharaonic title.
    from: the Khenti-Amentiu epithet dates to at least the First Dynasty, and was
      used as a pharaonic title.
  - when: 2686–2181 BC
    what: In the Old Kingdom, the pharaoh is considered a son of the sun god Ra
      who, after his death, ascended to join Ra in the sky.
    from: In the Old Kingdom (2686–2181 BC) the pharaoh was considered a son of
      the sun god Ra who, after his death, ascended to join Ra in the sky.
  - when: the beginning of the Fourth Dynasty
    what: The mortuary offering formula reads "An offering the king gives and
      Anubis".
    from: 'Amongst these mortuary texts, at the beginning of the Fourth Dynasty,
      is found: "An offering the king gives and Anubis".'
  - when: the middle of the Fifth Dynasty of Egypt (25th century BCE)
    what: The first evidence of the worship of Osiris, though it is likely he was
      worshipped much earlier.
    from: The first evidence of the worship of Osiris is from the middle of the
      Fifth Dynasty of Egypt (25th century BCE), though it is likely he was
      worshipped much earlier;
  - when: the end of the Fifth Dynasty
    what: The offering formula in all tombs becomes "An offering the king gives
      and Osiris".
    from: By the end of the Fifth Dynasty, the formula in all tombs becomes "An
      offering the king gives and Osiris".
  - when: about 1875 BC
    what: Ikhernofret erects his stela at Abydos, during the reign of Senwosret
      III; much of the extant information about the rites of Osiris is on it.
    from: Much of the extant information about the rites of Osiris can be found on
      the Ikhernofret Stela at Abydos erected in the Twelfth Dynasty by
      Ikhernofret, possibly a priest of Osiris or other official (the titles
      of Ikhernofret are described in his stela from Abydos) during the reign
      of Senwosret III (Pharaoh Sesostris, about 1875 BC).
  - when: ~1375 BC
    what: The version of the Book of the Dead in which Hunefer's heart is weighed
      and he is presented to Osiris.
    from: In the three scenes from the Book of the Dead (version from ~1375 BC)
      the dead man (Hunefer) is taken into the judgement hall by the
      jackal-headed Anubis.
  - when: thirteenth century BC
    what: A painting in the tomb of Pashedu shows a personified Eye of Horus
      offering incense to the enthroned Osiris.
    from: A personified Eye of Horus offers incense to the enthroned god Osiris in
      a painting from the tomb of Pashedu, thirteenth century BC
  - when: c. 595–525 BC
    what: The Brooklyn Museum's head of the god Osiris.
    from: Head of the God Osiris, c. 595–525 BC. Brooklyn Museum
  - when: the first century CE
    what: A fresco in the Temple of Isis at Pompeii depicts a ceremony worshipping
      the sarcophagus of Osiris.
    from: A ceremony worshipping the sarcophagus of Osiris, depicted in a fresco
      in the Temple of Isis at Pompeii from the first century CE.
  - when: the late 4th century
    what: Imperial decrees order the closing of temples to "pagan" gods.
    from: long after the imperial decrees of the late 4th century that ordered the
      closing of temples to "pagan" gods.
  - when: at least the 450s CE
    what: The cult of Isis and Osiris is still going at Philae, the last major
      ancient Egyptian temple to be closed.
    from: The cult of Isis and Osiris continued at Philae until at least the 450s
      CE, long after the imperial decrees of the late 4th century that ordered
      the closing of temples to "pagan" gods. Philae was the last major
      ancient Egyptian temple to be closed.
image-plan:
  - file: Standing_Osiris.svg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Osiris, lord of the dead and of rebirth. His green skin symbolizes
      regeneration, vegetation and fertility.
  - file: Osiris_-_God.png
    role: inline
    section: etymology-of-the-name
    caption: "The other colour he comes in: black skin, symbolizing the underworld
      deities and the fertility of the Nile floodplain."
  - file: Head_of_the_God_Osiris,_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg
    role: inline
    section: etymology-of-the-name
    caption: Head of the God Osiris, c. 595–525 BC. Brooklyn Museum.
  - file: Tumba_de_Tutankamón,_Valle_de_las_Reyes,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_75.jpg
    role: full
    section: appearance
    caption: On the north wall of Tutankhamun's burial chamber, the king, followed
      by his ka — an aspect of his soul — embraces Osiris.
  - file: La_Tombe_de_Horemheb_cropped.jpg
    role: inline
    section: father-of-horus
    caption: Osiris with Anubis and Horus, painted on the wall of the tomb of
      Horemheb (KV57).
  - file: Sokar-Osiris.svg
    role: inline
    section: father-of-horus
    caption: "Seker-Osiris, a syncretized god: the atef-crown, crook and flail
      belong to Osiris, the hawk head and was-sceptre to Seker."
  - file: Jewel_Osiris_family-E_6204-IMG_0641-gradient.jpg
    role: inline
    section: mythology
    caption: "The family in one jewel: Osiris on a lapis lazuli pillar, Horus on
      the left and Isis on the right (Twenty-second Dynasty, Louvre, Paris)."
  - file: Osirisinbudge.png
    role: inline
    section: worship
    caption: The names of Osiris run to six pages in Budge's 1920 hieroglyphic
      dictionary.
  - file: Tomb_TT3_of_Pashedu_(Kairoinfo4u).jpg
    role: inline
    section: worship
    caption: A personified Eye of Horus offers incense to the enthroned Osiris, in
      the tomb of Pashedu, thirteenth century BC.
  - file: Osiris-nepra.jpg
    role: inline
    section: death-and-institution-as-god-of-the-dead
    caption: Osiris-Nepra with wheat growing out of his body, from a bas-relief at
      Philae. The sprouting wheat implied resurrection.
  - file: Terre_cuite_pleureuse_Louvre_E27247.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ikhernofret-stela
    caption: A woman raising her right arm over her head, the typical gesture of
      mourning; this rare Egyptian terra cotta sculpture of the Eighteenth
      Dynasty may depict Isis mourning Osiris. Musée du Louvre, Paris.
  - file: The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg
    role: full
    section: judgement
    caption: "The judgement, in three scenes from a Book of the Dead of about 1375
      BC: jackal-headed Anubis leads the dead man Hunefer into the hall; his
      heart is weighed against the feather of Maat, with Ammit waiting the
      result and Thoth recording; Hunefer, having passed, is presented by
      falcon-headed Horus to Osiris, enthroned with Isis and Nephthys.
      (British Museum)"
  - file: Serapis_Pio-Clementino_Inv689_n2.jpg
    role: inline
    section: hellenization
    caption: Bust of Serapis, the god the early Ptolemaic kings promoted,
      portrayed in a Hellenistic form.
  - file: Pompeii_-_Temple_of_Isis_4_-_MAN.jpg
    role: inline
    section: hellenization
    caption: "Osiris in Italy: a first-century CE fresco in the Temple of Isis at
      Pompeii shows a ceremony worshipping his sarcophagus."
  - file: Philae,_seen_from_the_water,_Aswan,_Egypt,_Oct_2004.jpg
    role: full
    section: destruction-of-cult
    caption: The Philae temple on Agilkia Island, seen from the Nile. The cult of
      Isis and Osiris continued here until at least the 450s CE.
glossary:
  - term: Atef crown
    gloss: "The crown Osiris wears in his most developed iconography: the White
      crown of Upper Egypt with two curling ostrich feathers added at each
      side."
    from: Osiris is represented in his most developed form of iconography wearing
      the Atef crown, which is similar to the White crown of Upper Egypt, but
      with the addition of two curling ostrich feathers at each side.
  - term: Khenti-Amentiu
    gloss: A name of Osiris meaning "Foremost of the Westerners"; the epithet
      dates to at least the First Dynasty and was used as a pharaonic title.
    from: Osiris was the lord of the dead and the underworld, the "Lord of
      Silence" and Khenti-Amentiu, meaning "Foremost of the Westerners".
  - term: djed
    gloss: A type of pillar, usually understood as the backbone of Osiris.
    from: The djed, a type of pillar, was usually understood as the backbone of
      Osiris.
  - term: ba
    gloss: 'Not "soul" in the western sense: it has to do with power, reputation,
      force of character, especially in the case of a god.'
    from: Ba does not mean "soul" in the western sense, and has to do with power,
      reputation, force of character, especially in the case of a god.
  - term: Banebdjedet
    gloss: Osiris's ba worshipped in its own right, especially in the Delta city
      of Mendes, and depicted as a ram or ram-headed.
    from: This aspect of Osiris was referred to as Banebdjedet, which is
      grammatically feminine (also spelt "Banebded" or "Banebdjed"), literally
      "the ba of the lord of the djed, which roughly means The soul of the
      lord of the pillar.
  - term: '"Osiris Beds"'
    gloss: Beds formed in the shape of Osiris, filled with soil and sown with seed
      for the annual festival; the germinating seed symbolized Osiris rising
      from the dead.
    from: The annual festival involved the construction of "Osiris Beds" formed in
      shape of Osiris, filled with soil and sown with seed. The germinating
      seed symbolized Osiris rising from the dead.
  - term: imiut emblem
    gloss: An image of a stuffed, headless skin of an animal tied to a pole
      mounting a pot, associated with Osiris as god of the underworld and with
      Anubis, and sometimes included among a deceased person's funerary
      equipment.
    from: The imiut emblem- an image of a stuffed, headless skin of an animal tied
      to a pole mounting a pot, was a symbol associated both with Osiris as
      god of the underworld and with Anubis, god of mummification, was
      sometimes included among a deceased person's funerary equipment.
  - term: Ammit
    gloss: The soul-eating demon the guilty were thrown to, who did not share in
      eternal life.
    from: If found guilty, the person was thrown to the soul-eating demon Ammit
      and did not share in eternal life.
further:
  - slug: book-of-the-dead
    why: The judgement scene — the weighing of the heart, Ammit waiting the
      result, Hunefer presented to Osiris — comes out of it, and it is where
      the inward parts of Osiris are described.
  - slug: duat
    why: The underworld Osiris was lord of, and the kingdom the justified were
      welcomed into.
  - slug: egyptian-mythology
    why: The Osiris myth, in which Horus is conceived, is described as a central
      myth in ancient Egyptian belief.
anchors:
  hook: When his brother Set cut him to pieces after killing him, Osiris's
    sister-wife, Isis, with her sister Nephthys, searched Egypt to find each
    part of him. She collected all but one – Osiris's genitalia. She then
    wrapped his body up, enabling him to return to life.
  standfirst: The first evidence of the worship of Osiris is from the middle of
    the Fifth Dynasty of Egypt (25th century BCE), though it is likely he was
    worshipped much earlier; the Khenti-Amentiu epithet dates to at least the
    First Dynasty, and was used as a pharaonic title. Most information
    available on the Osiris myth is derived from allusions in the Pyramid
    Texts at the end of the Fifth Dynasty, later New Kingdom source documents
    such as the Shabaka Stone and "The Contendings of Horus and Seth", and
    much later, in the narratives of Greek authors including Plutarch and
    Diodorus Siculus.
image: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Standing_Osiris.svg/500px-Standing_Osiris.svg.png
images:
  - file: Osiris_-_God.png
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Osiris_-_God.png/250px-Osiris_-_God.png
    width: 1761
    height: 2746
