      underworld deities and fertility of the Nile floodplain.
  - file: Head_of_the_God_Osiris,_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Head_of_the_God_Osiris%2C_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg/250px-Head_of_the_God_Osiris%2C_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg
    width: 1164
    height: 1536
    caption: Head of the God Osiris, c. 595–525 BC. Brooklyn Museum
  - file: Tumba_de_Tutankamón,_Valle_de_las_Reyes,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_75.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Tumba_de_Tutankam%C3%B3n%2C_Valle_de_las_Reyes%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_75.jpg/500px-Tumba_de_Tutankam%C3%B3n%2C_Valle_de_las_Reyes%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_75.jpg
    width: 5877
    height: 2559
    caption: Scenes from the north wall of the burial chamber of Tutankhamun. On
      the left side, Tutankhamun, followed by his ka (an aspect of his soul),
      embraces Osiris.
  - file: La_Tombe_de_Horemheb_cropped.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/La_Tombe_de_Horemheb_cropped.jpg/250px-La_Tombe_de_Horemheb_cropped.jpg
    width: 372
    height: 514
    caption: The gods Osiris, Anubis, and Horus. Wall painting in the tomb of
      Horemheb (KV57).
  - file: Sokar-Osiris.svg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Sokar-Osiris.svg/250px-Sokar-Osiris.svg.png
    width: 350
    height: 823
    caption: The syncretized god Seker-Osiris. His iconography combines that of
      Osiris (atef-crown, crook and flail) and Seker (hawk head, was-sceptre).
  - file: Jewel_Osiris_family-E_6204-IMG_0641-gradient.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Jewel_Osiris_family-E_6204-IMG_0641-gradient.jpg/250px-Jewel_Osiris_family-E_6204-IMG_0641-gradient.jpg
    width: 7000
    height: 7000
    caption: The family of Osiris. Osiris on a lapis lazuli pillar in the middle,
      flanked by Horus on the left and Isis on the right (Twenty-second
      Dynasty, Louvre, Paris)
  - file: Osirisinbudge.png
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Osirisinbudge.png/250px-Osirisinbudge.png
    width: 1865
    height: 1246
    caption: Names of Osiris span six pages in Budge's 1920 hieroglyphic dictionary.
  - file: Tomb_TT3_of_Pashedu_(Kairoinfo4u).jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Tomb_TT3_of_Pashedu_%28Kairoinfo4u%29.jpg/250px-Tomb_TT3_of_Pashedu_%28Kairoinfo4u%29.jpg
    width: 2288
    height: 1728
    caption: A personified Eye of Horus offers incense to the enthroned god Osiris
      in a painting from the tomb of Pashedu, thirteenth century BC
  - file: Osiris-nepra.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Osiris-nepra.jpg/250px-Osiris-nepra.jpg
    width: 300
    height: 235
    caption: Osiris-Nepra, with wheat growing from his body. From a bas-relief at
      Philae. The sprouting wheat implied resurrection.
  - file: Terre_cuite_pleureuse_Louvre_E27247.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Terre_cuite_pleureuse_Louvre_E27247.jpg/250px-Terre_cuite_pleureuse_Louvre_E27247.jpg
    width: 2427
    height: 2832
    caption: A rare sample of Egyptian terra cotta sculpture which may depict Isis
      mourning Osiris. The sculpture, dated to the Eighteenth Dynasty,
      portrays a woman raising her right arm over her head, a typical gesture
      of mourning. Musée du Louvre, Paris.
  - file: The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg/500px-The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg
    width: 2446
    height: 1112
    caption: Judgment scene from the Book of the Dead. In the three scenes from
      the Book of the Dead (version from ~1375 BC) the dead man (Hunefer) is
      taken into the judgement hall by the jackal-headed Anubis. The next
      scene is the weighing of his heart against the feather of Maat, with
      Ammit waiting the result, and Thoth recording. Next, the triumphant
      Hunefer, having passed the test, is presented by the falcon-headed Horus
      to Osiris, seated in his shrine with Isis and Nephthys. (British Museum)
  - file: Serapis_Pio-Clementino_Inv689_n2.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Serapis_Pio-Clementino_Inv689_n2.jpg/250px-Serapis_Pio-Clementino_Inv689_n2.jpg
    width: 2350
    height: 3200
    caption: Bust of Serapis.
  - file: Pompeii_-_Temple_of_Isis_4_-_MAN.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Pompeii_-_Temple_of_Isis_4_-_MAN.jpg/250px-Pompeii_-_Temple_of_Isis_4_-_MAN.jpg
    width: 2609
    height: 2372
    caption: A ceremony worshipping the sarcophagus of Osiris, depicted in a
      fresco in the Temple of Isis at Pompeii from the first century CE.
  - file: Philae,_seen_from_the_water,_Aswan,_Egypt,_Oct_2004.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Philae%2C_seen_from_the_water%2C_Aswan%2C_Egypt%2C_Oct_2004.jpg/250px-Philae%2C_seen_from_the_water%2C_Aswan%2C_Egypt%2C_Oct_2004.jpg
    width: 1280
    height: 732
    caption: The Philae temple on Agilkia Island as seen from the Nile
